          "source": "def-based-string-link-and-labeled-link-peripheral-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $D^2$ be the oriented closed disk and give $C=D^2\\times[0,1]$ the product orientation. An $r$-component string link is an ordered family of pairwise disjoint smooth proper embeddings $\\tau_i:[0,1]\\to C$, for $1\\le i\\le r$, with fixed distinct endpoints $\\tau_i(0)=(p_i,0)$ and $\\tau_i(1)=(p_i,1)$ and fixed product collars at the endpoints. Orient each interval from bottom to top. The exterior $X_\\tau$ is obtained by removing the relative interiors of chosen pairwise disjoint product tubes around the intervals. The bottom and top punctured disks $P_-$ and $P_+$ are the copies of $D^2$ with the interiors of the endpoint tube disks removed.\n\nChoose a basepoint on the outer boundary of $P_-$, a path in the side boundary of $C$ to the corresponding top basepoint, and basing paths from these points to the component meridians. Each end inclusion, with the chosen connecting path for the top end, induces a based homomorphism $\\iota_\\pm:\\pi_1(P_\\pm)\\to\\pi_1(X_\\tau)$. For each string component choose a parallel path on its tube boundary from the bottom endpoint to the top endpoint, and choose access paths that close it to a based loop using the fixed side connector. The resulting element of $\\pi_1(X_\\tau)$ is its based string-link longitude; the parallel and access paths are part of the peripheral datum. The positively oriented boundaries of the endpoint tube disks are the designated meridian loops. Stacking two string links means rescaling them into the lower and upper halves of $C$ and identifying their matching endpoint collars; closure joins each top endpoint to the matching bottom endpoint outside $C$ and retains the component labels.\n\nFor a nonempty labeled oriented link $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r$ in the oriented three-sphere, choose pairwise disjoint closed product tubes $N(K_i)$, put $E_L=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}\\bigl(\\bigcup_iN(K_i)\\bigr)$, and write $T_i=\\partial N(K_i)$. A positive meridian $\\mu_i$ is the oriented boundary of a normal disk, with the disk orientation fixed by the rule that the oriented tangent of $K_i$ followed by the disk orientation gives the ambient orientation. A degree-one parallel is a simple closed curve on $T_i$ whose projection to $K_i$ has degree $+1$. A based longitude is the class of such a parallel transported to the common basepoint by a specified path. When the positive meridians form a basis of $H_1(E_L;\\mathbb Z)$, the self-meridian coordinate of a longitude is its coefficient on $[\\mu_i]$. A preferred zero-self-meridian longitude is a chosen degree-one parallel whose self-meridian coordinate is zero. The choice, when it exists, is part of the peripheral datum; this definition asserts neither existence nor uniqueness. Changing a common basing path conjugates the peripheral group elements simultaneously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-knot-meridian-and-preferred-longitude",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice as in the chosen tubular-neighbourhood data. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot with chosen exterior $E_K$ and boundary torus $T_K=\\partial E_K$, and let $p:T_K\\to K$ be the projection of the chosen normal-disk tube. Orient each normal disk so that its positive tangent direction followed by its oriented normal basis agrees with the orientation of $S^3$. A **meridian** $\\mu$ is the oriented boundary of one such normal disk. A **degree-one parallel curve** is a simple closed curve $\\lambda$ in $T_K$ for which $p|_\\lambda:\\lambda\\to K$ has degree $+1$. A **preferred longitude** is a degree-one parallel curve satisfying $i_*[\\lambda]=0$ in $H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z)$, where $i:T_K\\hookrightarrow E_K$ is inclusion. This is a condition on a curve; this definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness. Reversing the orientation of $K$ reverses the orientations of both $\\mu$ and any degree-one parallel curve. If a subgroup $P\\le\\pi_1(E_K,x)$ is specified using a path from $x$ to $T_K$, record it only up to replacing it by $gPg^{-1}$ for $g\\in\\pi_1(E_K,x)$.",
