        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-fundamental-group-of-finite-wedge-of-circles",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ be pointed at $[0]$, and for $r\\in\\mathbb N$ put\n\n$$W_r:=\\bigvee_{j<r}(Q,[0]).$$\n\nThen $\\pi_1(W_r,w)$ is the free group on the $r$ standard loops, one traversing each circle summand once. In particular it has rank $r$ ([[def-rank-of-a-finite-rank-free-group]]). For $r=0$, $W_0$ is a point and the basis is empty.",
