        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Let $D_i^+$ be disjoint disks slightly larger than the removed endpoint tube disks. The closed complement of their interiors lies in the interior of $P$ relative to $D^2$. By [F1], $H_*(D^2,P)$ is the direct sum of the relative groups of the $r$ disk pairs $(D_i^+,D_i^+\\setminus\\operatorname{int}D_i)$. Radial collars identify each pair with $(D^2,S^1)$. By [F2], [F3], and [F6], the relative group of this disk pair is $\\mathbb Z$ in degree $2$ and zero in every other degree. Its boundary map sends the relative disk to the positive endpoint meridian. Applying [F2] to $(D^2,P)$, with $D^2$ contractible by radial contraction, therefore gives $H_1(P)\\cong\\mathbb Z^r$ with that basis and $H_2(P)=0$.",
