        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Because the crossing set is finite, choose pairwise disjoint coordinate balls about its points, then shrink the product tubes so each crossing ball meets precisely the two prescribed local branches and contains no other tube. Shrinking a product tube radially is an isotopy of its boundary and leaves the exterior homeomorphic. Give $S^3$ a round metric and choose an embedded normal exponential collar of $\\Sigma$ of radius $\\rho>0$ below its injectivity radius. For $q\\in\\Sigma_L$, set $\\epsilon(q)=\\min\\{\\rho/4,d(q,L)/4\\}$. This is positive and continuous. The points $\\exp_q(t\\nu_q)$ with $|t|<\\epsilon(q)$ avoid $L$, since their distance from $q$ is less than $d(q,L)$. The variable-width collar $C=\\{\\exp_q(t\\nu_q):q\\in\\Sigma_L,\\ |t|<\\epsilon(q)\\}$ is embedded and open in $X$. Set $U=A_L\\cup C_{<0}$ and $V=B_L\\cup C_{>0}$, where $C_{<0}$ and $C_{>0}$ are its negative and positive normal halves. Then $U,V$ are open, cover $X$, and $U\\cap V=C$. Clamping the normal coordinate to $0$ retracts $U$ to $A_L$ and $V$ to $B_L$, while the collar itself retracts to $\\Sigma_L$. For the final assertion, choose a continuous function $\\chi:[0,1]\\to[0,1]$ equal to $1$ on $[0,1/4]$ and $0$ on $[1/2,1]$. On the positive collar of $A_L$, the homotopy $t\\mapsto t+s\\epsilon(q)\\chi(t/\\epsilon(q))/4$ ($0\\le s\\le1$) pushes every boundary point into $A_L^\\circ$, fixes points outside the collar, and stays below $\\epsilon(q)$; it stays in $X$ by the distance bound. It maps $A_L^\\circ$ to itself, so the inclusion $A_L^\\circ\\hookrightarrow A_L$ is a homotopy equivalence.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
