    "lem-magnus-commutator-degree-and-truncation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-lower-central-series-and-magnus-expansion-for-links",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For any group $G$, define its lower-central series by $\\Gamma_1G=G$ and\n$\\Gamma_{q+1}G=[G,\\Gamma_qG]$, where $[G,H]$ is the subgroup generated by\n$ghg^{-1}h^{-1}$ for $g\\in G$ and $h\\in H$. For $r\\ge1$, let\n$F_r=\\langle x_1,\\ldots,x_r\\rangle$ be the free group on the formal meridian\nsymbols $x_i$, with the free-group convention of its stated generators.\n\nLet $\\mathcal A_r=\\mathbb Z\\langle\\!\\langle X_1,\\ldots,X_r\\rangle\\!\\rangle$\nbe the ring of formal integer sums $\\sum_w a_wX_w$ over all finite words $w$\nin the letters $X_i$, with multiplication given by concatenation of words.\nThis is the degree-complete ring for the word-length filtration; a series with\ninvertible constant coefficient is a unit, with inverse determined recursively\ndegree by degree.\nFilter it by total degree (word length), and let $\\mathfrak m$ be the ideal of\nseries with zero constant term.\nThe Magnus expansion is the group homomorphism\n$$\nE:F_r\\longrightarrow\n\\mathbb Z\\langle\\!\\langle X_1,\\ldots,X_r\\rangle\\!\\rangle^\\times,\n\\qquad E(x_i)=1+X_i.\n$$\nIt sends $x_i^{-1}$ to $(1+X_i)^{-1}=\\sum_{k\\ge0}(-1)^kX_i^k$. For an\ninteger $d\\ge0$, calculations through degree $d$ use the truncated ring\n$R_d=\\mathbb Z\\langle X_1,\\ldots,X_r\\rangle/\\mathfrak m^{d+1}$. As a\n$\\mathbb Z$-module it has the finite monomial basis of words of length at most\n$d$. In $R_d$, the inverse of $1+A$ for $A\\in\\mathfrak m$ is the finite sum\n$1-A+A^2-\\cdots+(-A)^d$.\n\nFor a based string link, $x_1,\\ldots,x_r$ may be used as formal symbols\ncorresponding to the positive bottom-end meridians. No freeness or\nlower-central quotient isomorphism is asserted here; those properties require\nseparate proofs.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
