          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. The input is either (a) a based $r$-component string-link diagram\nwith its endpoint collars and product-tube parallels equal to the diagram's\nblackboard push-offs, or (b) a based finite regular\ndiagram of a closed $r$-component labeled oriented link with chosen product\ntubes, one initial strand on each component, and a common basepoint and access\npaths. The input also includes a maximum index length $N\\geq2$. The output is\nthe integer coefficients $\\mu_\\tau(I)$ in string-link mode, or the based\ncoefficients $\\mu_L^b(I)$, their lower indeterminacy data $\\Delta_I^b$, and\nresidues $\\bar\\mu_L^b(I)$ in closed-link mode, for every index sequence $I$\nwith $2\\leq |I|\\leq N$.\n\nSet $d=N-1$ and work in the truncated noncommutative ring\n$$\nR_d=\\mathbb Z\\langle X_1,\\ldots,X_r\\rangle/\\mathfrak m^{d+1},\n$$\nwhere $\\mathfrak m$ is the ideal generated by the letters. Carry out the\nfollowing finite procedure.\n\n1. Verify the finite crossing, orientation, over/under, and component data. If\n   a closed-link component has no underpassing point, insert a small type-I\n   curl on that component in a disk meeting no other component and record the\n   resulting based diagram. Give every strand $a$ the component label $j(a)$.\n\n2. For every strand $a$, initialize its meridian series by\n   $Y_a^{(1)}=1+X_{j(a)}$. For each degree $\\ell=2,\\ldots,d$, copy every\n   $Y_a^{(\\ell-1)}$ into the constant and lower-degree part of\n   $Y_a^{(\\ell)}$, with new degree-$\\ell$ terms initially zero, and reset each\n   chosen root strand to $1+X_j$. Then do one pass over each component in its\n   oriented order. In closed-link mode start at the chosen initial strand and\n   omit the final undercrossing back to that strand; in string-link mode start\n   at the bottom endpoint and use every undercrossing through to the top\n   endpoint. At an undercrossing with over-strand $o$ and source and target\n   under-arcs $s,t$, use the already available $Y_o^{(\\ell-1)}$ and the\n   current $Y_s^{(\\ell)}$ to set\n   $$\n   Y_t^{(\\ell)}=\\operatorname{trunc}_{\\ell}\\bigl((Y_o^{(\\ell-1)})^{-1}\n   Y_s^{(\\ell)}Y_o^{(\\ell-1)}\\bigr)\n   $$\n   when the traversal is source-to-target, and set\n   $$\n   Y_s^{(\\ell)}=\\operatorname{trunc}_{\\ell}\\bigl(Y_o^{(\\ell-1)}\n   Y_t^{(\\ell)}(Y_o^{(\\ell-1)})^{-1}\\bigr)\n   $$\n   when it is target-to-source. Here $\\operatorname{trunc}_{\\ell}$ discards\n   monomials of degree greater than $\\ell$, and every inverse is the finite\n   geometric series in the nilpotent ideal of $R_d$.\n\n3. For each component $j$, traverse its undercrossings in order and form\n   $W_j=\\prod_c (Y_{o(c)}^{(d)})^{\\epsilon_c}$, where the factors are in\n   traversal order and $\\epsilon_c=+1$ for source-to-target and $-1$ for\n   target-to-source. In string-link mode set $\\Lambda_j=W_j$. In closed-link\n   mode set\n   $$\n   \\Lambda_j=(1+X_j)^{-\\operatorname{wr}_j(D)}W_j,\n   $$\n   where $\\operatorname{wr}_j(D)$ is the sum of the determinant signs at\n   self-crossings of component $j$.\n\n4. For every $I=(i_1,\\ldots,i_k,j)$ with $1\\leq k\\leq d$, read the coefficient\n   of $X_{i_1}\\cdots X_{i_k}$ in $\\Lambda_j$. In string-link mode return this\n   integer. In closed-link mode, also compute the gcd of the coefficients for\n   all proper subsequences of cyclic permutations of $I$ of lengths at least\n   two, with empty gcd equal to zero, and return the corresponding residue\n   class.\n\nAll scans are over finite crossing, strand, degree, and index sets. The\nprocedure makes no polynomial-time claim. If a finite-input validation fails,\nreturn `invalid-input`.",
